ation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8b7bacbf.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Find single pixels on grid edges that are endpoints of lines, trace along the line to find connected closed regions, and fill those regions with the edge pixel's color.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"single colored pixels s on grid edge that are endpoints of a line\",\n  \"dst\": \"closed regions d connected to the line\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d is reachable by following the line from s\",\n  \"path\": \"trace along the continuous line from edge pixel s\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"fill interior of each closed region d encountered along path with s.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"d.interior.color = s.color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify single pixels on grid edge; Step 2: trace the line connected to each edge pixel; Step 3: for each closed region touching the line, flood-fill its interior with edge pixel color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"path-tracing\",\n    \"edge-detection\",\n    \"region-filling\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac6f9922\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4494\nmechanism=0.5690\nlexical=0.0124\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:draw_line, operation:ray_cast, operation:sort_order, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ac6f9922.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ac6f9922.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSummarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.\n\nInput structure\n- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot\n  backgrounds.\n- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of\n  slots, ordered by their positions.\n- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.\n\nRule\n- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch\n  the input edge.\n- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output\n  table.\n- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise\n  output the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are\n  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary\n  value.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"enclosed 4-connected background-color slot components arranged as a rectangular table, each containing zero or one non-background object color\",\n  \"transform\": \"collapse each slot to one output pixel in row/column order: use the contained object color if present, otherwise use the background color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"number of slot rows x number of slot columns\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b9e38dc0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4484\nmechanism=0.5551\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:grid_edge, operation:count, operation:draw_line, operation:flood_fill, operation:ray_cast, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b9e38dc0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/b9e38dc0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\nRule:\n- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n- Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\nKey insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"A pixel inside a half-surrounded area fills and radiates outward along the border opening, while isolated pixels move to grid edge in the radiate direction, leaving background trail\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"innermost connected same-color pixel s inside the half-surrounded inner area of a sprite\",\n  \"dst\": null,\n  \"match_cond\": \"\",\n  \"path\": \"fill the surrounded area and radiate along the surrounded border opening\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"multi_step\": \"\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"flood_fill\",\n    \"ray_casting\",\n    \"path_extension\",\n    \"obstacle\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"In the second transformation, the sprite s acts as an obstacle blocking the path of s2\",\n  \"type2\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src2\": \"isolated color pixel s2 away from the surrounded area bottleneck\",\n  \"dst2\": \"\",\n  \"match_cond2\": \"\",\n  \"path2\": \"straight line to the edge of the grid, same as the radiate direction\",\n  \"trajectory2\": \"bg color\",\n  \"transform2\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3998\nmechanism=0.4668\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:hole, operation:flood_fill, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructio